1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:03,000
An age-old battle.

2
00:00:03,001 --> 00:00:05,000
Good versus evil.

3
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:07,000
Criminal versus the law.

4
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:11,000
Now, a new player is balancing the scales of justice.

5
00:00:11,001 --> 00:00:13,000
Science.

6
00:00:13,001 --> 00:00:17,000
Hair follicles, micro fibers, DNA,

7
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:20,000
their unmasking murderers, solving old crimes,

8
00:00:20,001 --> 00:00:22,000
setting innocence free.

9
00:00:22,001 --> 00:00:26,000
Now, forensic science on modern marvels.

10
00:00:26,001 --> 00:00:40,001
The modern era of crime fighting began a century ago in the consulting room of a London physician.

11
00:00:44,560 --> 00:00:49,600
With his medical practice failing, Dr. Arthur Conan Doyle turned to writing stories.

12
00:00:50,800 --> 00:00:55,400
In 1887, he invented the most famous fictional character of all time.

13
00:00:56,000 --> 00:00:59,700
and with him a new scientific field.

14
00:00:59,701 --> 00:01:04,240
Consulting detective Sherlock Holmes deciphered physical clues,

15
00:01:04,241 --> 00:01:08,740
a revolutionary approach to solving crimes.

16
00:01:08,741 --> 00:01:09,840
What do you think?

17
00:01:09,841 --> 00:01:11,640
There's been murdered done.

18
00:01:11,641 --> 00:01:13,960
The murderer was a man.

19
00:01:13,961 --> 00:01:17,860
He was in the prime of life, six feet in height,

20
00:01:17,861 --> 00:01:21,200
has small feet wear square-toed boots,

21
00:01:21,201 --> 00:01:25,900
limped slightly on the left foot, and smokes Trichinopley cigars.

22
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:28,900
The early police work was totally dependent on informers.

23
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:30,800
And then Arthur Conan Doyle came along,

24
00:01:30,900 --> 00:01:32,800
and he said, you don't need informers.

25
00:01:32,900 --> 00:01:34,600
You don't need to hit people over the head

26
00:01:34,700 --> 00:01:36,500
with rubber pipes to make them confess.

27
00:01:36,600 --> 00:01:38,100
They're going to leave evidence behind.

28
00:01:42,200 --> 00:01:45,200
Once Sherlock Holmes showed what was possible,

29
00:01:45,300 --> 00:01:48,600
police officers at Scotland Yard followed his lead.

30
00:01:49,800 --> 00:01:52,000
Forensic science was born.

31
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:03,000
19th century policemen struggled with the difficulty

32
00:02:03,001 --> 00:02:10,000
of reliably identifying individuals, photography, body measurements,

33
00:02:10,001 --> 00:02:13,000
even branding offenders.

34
00:02:13,001 --> 00:02:16,000
All these methods fell short.

35
00:02:16,001 --> 00:02:20,000
Then in the 1890s, British scientists developed the long-sought,

36
00:02:20,001 --> 00:02:24,000
full-proof means of identification, fingerprints.

37
00:02:24,001 --> 00:02:28,000
They organize the seemingly random patterns

38
00:02:28,001 --> 00:02:33,000
into three categories, loops, arches, and swirls,

39
00:02:33,001 --> 00:02:37,000
allowing prints to be classified and compared.

40
00:02:37,001 --> 00:02:44,000
By 1903, both Scotland Yard and the New York City Police Department

41
00:02:44,001 --> 00:02:46,000
fingerprinted suspects.

42
00:02:46,001 --> 00:02:49,000
A practice quickly adopted the world over.

43
00:02:49,001 --> 00:02:53,000
In the decades to come, even public enemy number one

44
00:02:53,001 --> 00:02:58,000
one would learn the power of the new technology.

45
00:02:58,001 --> 00:03:00,500
John Dillinger, Pudlum, Bandit,

46
00:03:00,501 --> 00:03:02,500
jailbreaker, bank robber and murderer

47
00:03:02,501 --> 00:03:05,500
was headed for Indiana to be tried for murder.

48
00:03:05,501 --> 00:03:07,500
A shudder of relief thrilled the country,

49
00:03:07,501 --> 00:03:10,500
for the entire nation had hoped for the capture of this gunman,

50
00:03:10,501 --> 00:03:14,500
who, from petty obscurity, had leaped to shameful notoriety

51
00:03:14,501 --> 00:03:17,500
as public enemy number one within a few brief weeks.

52
00:03:21,500 --> 00:03:22,500
Dillinger's wild rampage of Rampage

53
00:03:22,501 --> 00:03:26,000
of robberies and murders had terrorized the Midwest.

54
00:03:26,001 --> 00:03:30,500
Awaiting trial he was held in a supposedly escape-proof jail

55
00:03:30,501 --> 00:03:34,500
and guarded by an army of gun-toting FBI men.

56
00:03:34,501 --> 00:03:38,500
He boldly bluffed his way out with a wooden gun

57
00:03:38,501 --> 00:03:40,500
and was back on the loose.

58
00:03:40,501 --> 00:03:43,500
Desperate to conceal his identity, he dyed his hair,

59
00:03:43,501 --> 00:03:48,500
grew a mustache, and had a surgeon alter his face and his fingertips.

60
00:03:48,501 --> 00:03:51,500
Throughout history, people have the people of the United States.

61
00:03:51,501 --> 00:03:54,300
Throughout history, people have tried to obscure or destroy

62
00:03:54,400 --> 00:03:55,300
their fingerprints.

63
00:03:55,400 --> 00:03:57,800
There's a great legendary story of John Dillinger,

64
00:03:57,900 --> 00:04:02,140
who paid a doctor $5,000 to treat his fingerprints with acid.

65
00:04:02,240 --> 00:04:05,240
He did successfully obscure the center of the fingerprints,

66
00:04:05,340 --> 00:04:07,040
but there were ridges around those points

67
00:04:07,140 --> 00:04:09,160
that were easily identifiable as his.

68
00:04:11,560 --> 00:04:15,360
For months, Dillinger successfully eluded a nationwide manhunt,

69
00:04:16,660 --> 00:04:19,060
then betrayed by one of his many girlfriends,

70
00:04:19,160 --> 00:04:21,400
he was gunned down by G-men,

71
00:04:21,500 --> 00:04:26,000
as he emerged from the biograph theater in Chicago.

72
00:04:26,001 --> 00:04:28,500
The bullet-riddled corpse bore little resemblance

73
00:04:28,501 --> 00:04:31,040
to a figure known by all.

74
00:04:31,041 --> 00:04:36,140
But the outer ridges of his fingers proved that it was, John Dillinger.

75
00:04:46,100 --> 00:04:48,200
Forty years later, the next generation

76
00:04:48,201 --> 00:04:51,500
of fingerprint technology would expose one of the centuries most

77
00:04:51,501 --> 00:04:53,500
notorious assassins.

78
00:05:01,500 --> 00:05:05,680
On April 4th, 1968, civil rights leader Martin Luther King, Jr.

79
00:05:05,681 --> 00:05:11,120
was shot to death on the balcony of a Memphis motel.

80
00:05:11,121 --> 00:05:16,000
In a room across the street, police recovered the killer's rifle.

81
00:05:16,001 --> 00:05:21,000
Nearly wiped clean, the rifle butt contained the partial print of a single finger.

82
00:05:21,001 --> 00:05:28,000
With few other leads, a massive effort was made to identify the print.

83
00:05:28,001 --> 00:05:40,000
The FBI examiners literally broke up into groups of thousands of individuals, and one by one, they went through those fingerprint cards,

84
00:05:40,001 --> 00:05:49,000
manually searching the fingerprints found at that scene against millions of card files.

85
00:05:49,001 --> 00:05:54,000
But the FBI files contained over 50 million prints.

86
00:05:54,001 --> 00:05:57,000
Too many for any manual search.

87
00:05:57,001 --> 00:06:01,000
For the first time in fingerprinting history,

88
00:06:01,001 --> 00:06:04,000
a rudimentary computer narrowed the field.

89
00:06:04,001 --> 00:06:08,000
It selected out the fingerprint cards of wanted felons,

90
00:06:08,001 --> 00:06:11,000
a manageable 2,000 names.

91
00:06:11,001 --> 00:06:14,000
They were going to go through every one of those 2,000 cards

92
00:06:14,001 --> 00:06:16,000
and see if they could make a match.

93
00:06:16,001 --> 00:06:18,000
Just after they started, somebody said,

94
00:06:18,001 --> 00:06:20,040
somebody said, I got him.

95
00:06:20,041 --> 00:06:22,000
And everybody laughed and assumed he was kidding.

96
00:06:22,001 --> 00:06:23,040
He said, no, I'm not kidding.

97
00:06:23,041 --> 00:06:24,040
This is him.

98
00:06:24,041 --> 00:06:25,040
This is the guy.

99
00:06:25,041 --> 00:06:27,340
It was maybe the third or fourth car they looked at,

100
00:06:27,341 --> 00:06:30,340
and it was James Earl Ray, and that's how he was identified.

101
00:06:30,341 --> 00:06:35,960
Ray's prints were traced to maps, hotel rooms,

102
00:06:35,961 --> 00:06:39,660
and car keys across the south.

103
00:06:39,661 --> 00:06:42,600
Investigators followed a trail of fake IDs

104
00:06:42,601 --> 00:06:45,360
from city to city and across the Atlantic.

105
00:06:45,361 --> 00:06:59,361
Finally, the FBI and Interpol tracked him to London, where he was apprehended.

106
00:07:04,140 --> 00:07:08,480
Ray pleaded guilty to killing King and was sentenced to 99 years in prison.

107
00:07:15,360 --> 00:07:20,360
In recent years, computers have evolved into an essential part

108
00:07:20,361 --> 00:07:23,360
of fingerprinting technology.

109
00:07:23,361 --> 00:07:25,360
The automated fingerprint identification system,

110
00:07:25,361 --> 00:07:30,360
or A-F-I-S, compares the ridges and swirls of millions

111
00:07:30,361 --> 00:07:33,360
